Task: Resolve the sticky/overflow conflict in the payments table
Track: rent_fy_grouping_20260907 — Phase 2
Verification tier: 3 (presentational)

WHAT CHANGED
components/rental-payments-section.tsx: the table wrapper changed from
'overflow-x-auto' to 'max-sm:overflow-x-auto', with a comment recording why.

WHY — THE NON-OBVIOUS CONSTRAINT IN THIS TRACK
Per the CSS Overflow module, when one axis is 'visible' and the other is not,
the visible one computes to 'auto'. So a wrapper carrying only overflow-x:auto
is a scroll container on BOTH axes. A position:sticky row inside it anchors to
that wrapper rather than the viewport, and because the wrapper is only as tall
as its content it never scrolls vertically — so the year divider would render
perfectly and simply never pin. This fails silently, which is why the plan
isolated it as its own task ahead of building the divider.

Confirmed empirically in Chrome rather than assumed:
  d1.style.overflowX = 'auto'  ->  computed { overflowX: 'auto', overflowY: 'auto' }
  control div (no overflow)    ->  computed { overflowX: 'visible', overflowY: 'visible' }

RESOLUTION TAKEN
The scroll container is confined to widths below sm, which is the fallback the
spec pre-approved (FR5). At sm and above there is no scroll container, so the
divider pins to the page as intended. Below sm the wrapper still provides
horizontal scrolling for this five-column table — satisfying the workflow rule
that tables scroll within their container rather than the page — and the
divider degrades to a plain, non-sticky row, which FR5 permits.

Removing the wrapper outright was rejected: the payments table has five columns
(Payment, Date, Period, Gross rent, actions) against the expenses table's four,
so it genuinely needs horizontal scroll on a phone. Hiding the Period column on
mobile was rejected as it removes data the user recorded.

